Okay, to fill that gap, here's a quick-shot (without knowing any
specific prerequisites, so just defining some)...

A shell solution (should work in ksh, bash, and with only little
change also in zsh) could be...

  # select from files
  set -- *.nhrc
  select sel in "${@%.nhrc}"
  do  [[ -n "${sel}" ]] && break
  done
  # construct a nethackrc file
  { cat nhrc.nethackrc
    printf "OPTIONS=name:%s,role:%s,race:%s,align:%s,gender:%s\n" ${sel//-/ }
    cat "${sel}.nhrc"
  } > "${HOME}"/.nethackrc
  # start nethack
  nethack

This program, let's call it nhrc, assumes a couple files like, say

  Explore-random-random-random-random.nhrc
  Female-random-random-random-fem.nhrc
  Janis-cav-hum-law-mal.nhrc
  Janis-sam-hum-law-random.nhrc
  Janis-wiz-random-random-random.nhrc

Each of those files contain specific Nethack option config lines.
Another file called nhrc.nethackrc contains the nethack options
and served as template where the specific options shall be added.
In case that the options should not be added, rather that they
should replace existing options, you would have to remove those
options prior adding them. A solution is to replace the command

  cat nhrc.nethackrc

by a command like

  sed 's/\([=,]\)\(name\|role\|race\|align\|gender\):[[:alnum:]_]*/\1/g'
nhrc.nethackrc

Of course you can tweak that script, e.g. to assert that the user
has chosen only valid file names, or file names that conform to
existing combinations[*] only (e.g. allow no male valkyries), or
to do syntax checking and allow only valid options; this is left
as an exercise for the reader. :-)

